    summary: `# 🦠 Zoonoses #1.1 — Rickettsial Diseases

## 🎯 Pathogen overview
- **Order:** Rickettsiales — obligate intracellular Gram-negative
- **Genera:** Rickettsia, Orientia, Ehrlichia, Anaplasma, Neorickettsia
- **Vector-borne** (ticks, mites, fleas, lice)

## 🦟 Major rickettsial zoonoses

| Disease | Pathogen | Vector | Reservoir |
|---|---|---|---|
| Scrub typhus | *Orientia tsutsugamushi* | Chigger mite | Rodents |
| Murine typhus | *R. typhi* | Flea | Rats |
| Spotted fever group | *R. conorii*, *R. rickettsii* | Tick | Dogs, rodents |
| Q fever | *Coxiella burnetii* | Tick + aerosol | Cattle, sheep, goats |
| Anaplasmosis | *Anaplasma phagocytophilum* | Ixodes tick | Deer, rodents |
| Ehrlichiosis | *Ehrlichia chaffeensis* | Lone star tick | Deer |

## 🇹🇭 Thailand context
- **Scrub typhus** = MOST common rickettsial dz in TH
- Eschar (black necrotic ulcer at chigger bite site) — pathognomonic
- Northern + NE Thailand endemic — rice paddies, scrub
- ~10-30% mortality if untreated

## 🩺 Clinical features
- Fever + headache + myalgia (nonspecific!)
- Eschar (scrub typhus)
- Maculopapular rash (spotted fever)
- Hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy

## 🔬 Diagnosis
- **Weil-Felix test** — historical, low specificity
- **IFA** (gold standard) — paired serum
- **PCR** — eschar, blood
- **Culture** — BSL-3, rarely done

## 💊 Treatment
- **Doxycycline** = first-line (ALL rickettsial dz)
- 7-14 days
- Pediatric/pregnancy: chloramphenicol or azithromycin

## 🎯 Q fever (special)
- *Coxiella burnetii* — spore-like, environmentally stable
- Aerosol transmission (parturition products)
- Veterinary occupational hazard
- Acute = flu-like; chronic = endocarditis

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ Scrub typhus = Orientia tsutsugamushi (chigger)
2. ⭐ Eschar = pathognomonic for scrub typhus
3. ⭐ Doxycycline = treatment of choice (ALL rickettsial)
4. ⭐ Q fever vet occupation hazard (parturition aerosol)
5. ⭐ Northern/NE TH endemic for scrub typhus`,
