    summary: `# 🦠 Zoonoses #8.1.1 — Bacterial Zoonoses I

## 🎯 Major bacterial zoonoses (Part I)

| Disease | Pathogen | Reservoir | Transmission |
|---|---|---|---|
| Brucellosis | *Brucella* spp. | Cattle/goat/swine | Milk, abortion fluids, contact |
| Tuberculosis | *M. bovis* | Cattle | Milk, aerosol |
| Leptospirosis | *Leptospira* spp. | Rats, dogs, cattle | Water, urine |
| Salmonellosis | *Salmonella* spp. | Poultry, reptiles | Foodborne |
| Campylobacteriosis | *Campylobacter jejuni* | Poultry | Foodborne |
| Listeriosis | *L. monocytogenes* | Cattle, env | Soft cheese, deli |
| Anthrax | *Bacillus anthracis* | Cattle, sheep, env | Contact, spores |
| Plague | *Yersinia pestis* | Rodents | Flea (Xenopsylla) |

## 🐄 Brucellosis
- *B. abortus* (cattle), *B. melitensis* (goat — most virulent), *B. suis* (pig), *B. canis* (dog)
- "Undulant fever" in humans
- Vet occupational hazard #1
- Cattle: abortion (3rd trimester), retained placenta, infertility
- Human: fever, sweats, arthralgia, hepatosplenomegaly, orchitis
- Dx: rose Bengal test, ELISA, culture (BSL-3)
- Tx: doxycycline + rifampin or streptomycin (6 weeks)
- Control (cattle): test-and-slaughter, S19 vaccine
- TH: *B. melitensis* in goats (NE), DLD eradication program

## 🐄 Bovine TB
- *Mycobacterium bovis* (M. tuberculosis complex)
- Cattle reservoir, also wildlife (badger UK, possum NZ)
- Transmission: aerosol cattle-cattle, raw milk to humans
- Cattle: granulomas (lung, lymph node), wasting
- Humans: extrapulmonary TB common (cervical lymph node, GI)
- Dx (cattle): caudal fold tuberculin test (CFT) + comparative cervical test
- Control: test-and-slaughter, milk pasteurization, AHRU/CU vet team
- TH: tested in dairy herds, sporadic

## 💧 Leptospirosis ("ไข้หนู")
- *Leptospira interrogans* (>250 serovars)
- Reservoir: rats (*L. icterohaemorrhagiae*), dogs (*canicola*), cattle (*hardjo*)
- Transmission: urine-contaminated water (skin abrasions, mucosa)
- High risk: rice farmers, flood survivors, vets, slaughterhouse workers
- Clinical: biphasic — septicemic (fever, myalgia) → immune (jaundice, AKI, hemorrhage = Weil's)
- Dx: MAT (gold std), PCR, dark-field microscopy
- Tx: doxycycline (mild), penicillin (severe)
- Prevention: vaccinate dogs/cattle, rodent control, PPE
- TH: peak in rainy season, NE flood areas

## 🐔 Salmonellosis
- *S. enterica* (>2,500 serovars)
- *S. Typhimurium*, *S. Enteritidis* — poultry, eggs (most common)
- *S. Typhi* — human only, typhoid fever
- Reptiles (turtles, iguanas) → carrier
- Human: gastroenteritis (self-limiting), bacteremia (immunocompromised)
- Dx: stool culture, blood culture
- Tx: rehydration; antibiotics only for severe
- Control: cook eggs, refrigerate, no raw poultry, hand hygiene

## 🐔 Campylobacteriosis
- *C. jejuni* (most common bacterial gastroenteritis worldwide!)
- Poultry intestine — carcass contamination
- Self-limiting diarrhea, can trigger Guillain-Barré syndrome
- Tx: rehydration; azithromycin if severe

## 🧀 Listeriosis
- *L. monocytogenes* — soil, ruminant abortion, soft cheese, deli meat
- Cattle: abortion, encephalitis (silage)
- Human: meningitis (newborn, elderly, immunocompromised), pregnancy loss
- Tx: ampicillin + gentamicin

## 🐂 Anthrax
- *B. anthracis* — spore-forming, soil-stable
- Cutaneous (most common), pulmonary (woolsorter's), GI
- Cattle/sheep sudden death, dark blood (no clot)
- DO NOT NECROPSY (spore release)
- Tx: ciprofloxacin or doxycycline + 60-day course
- TH: sporadic outbreaks, vaccinate livestock in endemic areas

## 🐀 Plague
- *Y. pestis* — rodent-flea-human cycle
- Bubonic (lymph node), septicemic, pneumonic
- Madagascar, US Southwest, Mongolia
- Tx: streptomycin or doxycycline
- TH: not endemic currently

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ Brucella melitensis = most virulent in humans (goat)
2. ⭐ Leptospirosis: rats reservoir, MAT gold std, doxycycline
3. ⭐ Anthrax: do NOT necropsy (spore release)
4. ⭐ Listeria: pregnant women avoid soft cheese, deli
5. ⭐ Bovine TB: pasteurize milk, CFT skin test`,
