    examFormat: "MCQ + study design + outbreak",
    summary: `# 🦠 Zoonoses #10 — Epidemiological Approach

## 🎯 Why epi for zoonoses?
- Multi-host (human + animal + environment)
- Surveillance gaps (animal underreporting)
- Outbreak detection requires One Health
- Risk factors often occupational/behavioral
- Spatial-temporal clustering common

## 📊 Study designs (zoonoses application)

| Design | Use | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Descriptive | Hypothesis generation | Dengue case map |
| Cross-sectional | Prevalence | Brucella seroprevalence in cattle |
| Case-control | Risk factor (rare dz) | Strep suis vs raw pork |
| Cohort | Incidence + RR | Vet students rabies PrEP cohort |
| Ecological | Population-level | Climate + vector-borne dz |
| Mathematical model | Transmission dynamics | R0 for COVID-19 |
| Spatial | Cluster detection | Avian flu hotspots |

## 🔬 Key metrics for zoonoses

### R0 (basic reproduction number)
- Avg new cases from 1 case in fully susceptible population
- R0 < 1 → outbreak fades
- R0 > 1 → outbreak grows
- Examples: COVID-19 ~2-3, measles ~12-18, rabies ~1.1

### Attack rate
- Cases / population at risk during outbreak
- Outbreak investigation backbone

### Force of infection (λ)
- Rate at which susceptibles become infected
- Age-specific seroprevalence → estimate λ

### CFR (case fatality rate)
- Deaths / cases (e.g., rabies 100%, MERS 35%, H5N1 ~50%)

## 🔍 Outbreak investigation steps
1. Confirm outbreak (count vs baseline)
2. Verify diagnosis
3. Define case (clinical + lab + time + place)
4. Find cases (active surveillance, line list)
5. Descriptive epi (epi curve, map, by demographics)
6. Hypothesize source/mode
7. Test hypothesis (case-control, environmental sampling)
8. Implement control measures
9. Communicate (risk communication)
10. Final report + lessons learned

## 🌡️ Surveillance types
- **Passive:** clinician/farmer reporting (low sensitivity)
- **Active:** systematic case-finding (resource-intensive)
- **Sentinel:** designated sites (cost-effective)
- **Syndromic:** symptom-based (early warning)
- **Genomic:** sequencing-based (variant tracking)
- **One Health:** integrated human + animal + env

## 🇹🇭 Thailand zoonoses surveillance
- **DDC** (Disease Control, MOH) — human cases
- **DLD** (Livestock Development, MOAC) — animal
- **DNP** (National Parks) — wildlife
- **OneHealth Thailand** platform — coordination
- WOAH/WAHIS reporting

## 📝 Risk analysis framework (WOAH)
- Hazard identification → Risk assessment → Risk mgmt → Risk communication
- Used for import risk, food safety, AMR

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ R0 < 1 = outbreak fades; > 1 = grows
2. ⭐ Outbreak investigation = 10 steps (confirm → control → report)
3. ⭐ Active vs passive vs sentinel surveillance
4. ⭐ DDC + DLD + DNP = TH One Health
5. ⭐ Case definition = essential for outbreak`,
